            <ArticleTitle>Use of fibrinogen and thrombin sponge in pediatric split liver transplantation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>673-679</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0102-86502017000800673</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1590/s0102-865020170080000009</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="Purpose:" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">To analyze the use of this sponge in pediatric patients undergoing split-liver transplantation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Methods:" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Retrospective study, including 35 pediatric patients undergoing split-liver transplantation, divided into two groups according to the use of the sponge: 18 patients in Group A (no sponge) and 17 in Group B (with sponge).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Results:" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">The characteristics of recipients and donors were similar. We observed greater number of reoperation due to bleeding in the wound area in Group A (10 patients - 55.5%) than in Group B (3 patients - 17.6%); p = 0.035. The median volume of red blood cells transfused in Group A was significantly higher (73.4 ± 102.38 mL/kg) than that in Group B (35.1 ± 41.67 mL/kg); p = 0.048. Regarding bile leak there was no statistical difference.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Conclusion:" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">The use of the human fibrinogen and thrombin sponge, required lower volume of red blood cell transfusion and presented lower reoperation rates due to bleeding in the wound area.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
